{
  "id": "COMPOUND:31899 - PROTEIN:5802",
  "verbalization": "Nortoxiferine, dihydro- is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hs746T through a series of target-mediated pathways. The evidence suggests that the compound acts as a target of multiple compounds, all of which ultimately target the 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14. This subunit is a key component of the proteasome, an essential complex responsible for protein degradation. The 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14 is known to be involved in the regulation of proteasome activity, and its inhibition can lead to the accumulation of damaged proteins, which may contribute to disease progression.\n\nThe presence of multiple compounds that target the same subunit, including Diplotrin A and Pseudopterosin Q, indicates that the 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14 is a critical node in the biochemical network that connects Nortoxiferine, dihydro- to Hs746T. This suggests that the compound may exert its effects through a common mechanism, potentially involving the modulation of proteasome function. The integration of these pathways supports the hypothesis that Nortoxiferine, dihydro- influences Hs746T through the regulation of the proteasome, which in turn may affect cellular processes critical to the disease state. The evidence is consistent with a mechanistic link between the compound and the target, though further experimental validation is required to confirm the exact role of the proteasome in this relationship.",
